{# How a generated page links THE STYLESHEET — local first, CDN if the local
   copy is absent. It lives beside the bundle it loads; js/js.loader.html.j2 is
   its twin, and both shells include the pair:

     {% include "css/css.loader.html.j2" %}
     {% include "js/js.loader.html.j2" %}

   `local_href` is the relative path from WHERE THE FILE IS WRITTEN back to the
   skill — a showcase three folders deep gets `../../..`, a report in docs/ gets
   whatever reaches the skill from there. It is empty when no relative path
   exists (a different Windows drive), and then only the CDN is linked.

   WHY BOTH. Local first, so a page previews the CURRENT tree the moment it is
   generated — no publish, no cache, no version bump between editing a
   stylesheet and seeing it. CDN second, so the same file still renders once it
   leaves the tree: copied elsewhere, emailed, opened from a download folder.
   The fallback is what makes a generated page portable AND live.

   HOW THE FALLBACK FIRES, and why this half is the easy one. `onerror` on the
   tag itself — the browser tells us the local file is missing, so nothing has
   to poll or guess, and a page whose local assets are present pays nothing. A
   stylesheet can retarget the SAME element, because a <link> re-fetches when
   its href changes. `this.onerror=null` first, so a CDN that is also
   unreachable cannot loop. The script half cannot do this — see its file. #}
{% if local_href %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ local_href }}/css/bundle.css"
        onerror="this.onerror=null;this.href='{{ cdn_href }}/css/bundle.css'">
{% else %}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ cdn_href }}/css/bundle.css">
{% endif %}
